Assess whether the following migration PoC provides useful evidence for proceeding and design the cheapest decisive follow-up if needed. Use only the supplied synthetic artifacts; no execution is available.

Migration contract: preserve every account ID, email, status, and integer creditCents exactly, including negative balances; produce exactly one target account per source account. Source and target currently use the same field names. The submitted design claims this migration is lossless.

Actual migration core: function migrate(rows) { return rows.map(r => ({ id: r.id, email: r.email, status: r.status, creditCents: 0 })); }
Verifier core: const project = r => [r.id, r.email, r.status]; const digest = rows => sha256(JSON.stringify(rows.map(project))); const passed = source.length === target.length && digest(source) === digest(target);

Synthetic run record: verifier returned passed=true for 3 rows. Source snapshot: [{id:'ac1',email:'one@example.test',status:'active',creditCents:1200},{id:'ac2',email:'two@example.test',status:'paused',creditCents:-500},{id:'ac3',email:'three@example.test',status:'active',creditCents:0}]. Independently captured target snapshot: [{id:'ac1',email:'one@example.test',status:'active',creditCents:0},{id:'ac2',email:'two@example.test',status:'paused',creditCents:0},{id:'ac3',email:'three@example.test',status:'active',creditCents:0}]. The author proposes adding more random rows and reusing the existing digest as expected truth. What should we conclude, and what evidence would be worth collecting next? Do not expand this into a new migration platform.
